<h2>Where the receiver sits among interim remedies</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Remedy</th><th>What it does</th><th>Limit</th></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary injunction (Order XXXIX)</td><td>Forbids dealings — alienation, demolition, dispossession</td><td>Preserves the status quo but leaves the asset, and its income, with the party in control</td></tr>
<tr><td>Attachment before judgment (Order XXXVIII Rule 5)</td><td>Secures the asset against disposal to defeat a future decree</td><td>Protects the decree, not the asset's management or income</td></tr>
<tr><td>Receiver (Order XL)</td><td>Transfers custody and management to a court officer who collects income and preserves value</td><td>Harshest of the three; granted only where lesser remedies will not protect the property</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The comparison explains the courts' caution. An injunction leaves possession undisturbed; a receiver displaces it. That is why the five classic considerations — strong prima facie case, imminent danger of loss, conduct of the applicant, comparative hardship, and whether the appointment would effectively hand over the fruits of the suit before trial — are applied with rigour, and why consent receivers (both sides agreeing on neutral management) pass much more easily than contested ones.</p>

<h2>The situations that persuade Delhi courts</h2>
<p>Receivers are most readily appointed where the property produces income whose collection by one party would be irreversible in practice: tenanted commercial buildings during title disputes, with the receiver collecting rent and depositing it in court; estates of deceased owners fought over by heirs, pending probate or partition; partnership businesses in dissolution, where continued one-sided control dissipates goodwill and stock; and mortgage enforcement where the security is wasting. In execution, the receiver is the standard answer to judgment-debtors whose assets are income streams rather than attachable things — a running shop, a licensed premises, a toll or lease. By contrast, courts resist receivers over owner-occupied residences and over businesses merely because the defendant is litigating; dispossession is not a case-management tool.</p>

<h2>Running the receivership</h2>
<p>The appointing order is the receiver's constitution, and drafting it well avoids a year of applications: it should define the property, enumerate powers (rent collection, inventory, repairs, insurance, bank account, legal proceedings), fix security and remuneration, set the accounting cycle, and direct where income is deposited. The receiver's accounts are filed in court and open to both parties' scrutiny; objections are decided by the appointing court. Parties remain free to apply for directions — to permit a lease, sanction repairs, or sell perishable stock — and the receiver equally may seek the court's guidance in doubt. The discipline of Rule 4 completes the structure: a receiver who fails to account or occasions loss by wilful default answers personally, and the security can be proceeded against.</p>

<h2>Strategy notes</h2>
<p>For applicants: come with arithmetic, not adjectives. Rent rolls, diverted collections, municipal dues mounting, insurance lapsed — concrete numbers establish the danger that the legal standard demands. Propose a named, neutral receiver with a workable fee, and accept safeguards (photographic inventory, joint-signature accounts) that reduce the intrusion. For respondents: the best answer to a receiver application is usually an undertaking that removes the danger — depositing rents in court, maintaining accounts, giving inspection — because the remedy is premised on necessity, and a credible lesser protection defeats necessity. And for both: remember that receivership is expensive and slow to unwind; in commercial relationships with any future, a consent arrangement under the court's supervision almost always beats a contested custodian.</p>

